WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.865
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.865 --> 00:00:05.365
I was diagnosed with dystonia when

00:00:05.365 --> 00:00:06.231
I was 12.

00:00:06.231 --> 00:00:07.883
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.883 --> 00:00:10.185
Dystonia is a neurological disease

00:00:10.185 --> 00:00:12.488
that basically means that some part

00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:14.405
of my brain is just a little bit

00:00:14.405 --> 00:00:15.107
unusual.

00:00:15.107 --> 00:00:16.528
For me, it was in one leg,

00:00:16.528 --> 00:00:18.094
then it was in my right hand.

00:00:18.094 --> 00:00:19.517
And then it was in my left leg.

00:00:19.517 --> 00:00:20.995
And then I had tremors.

00:00:20.995 --> 00:00:23.616
And then my speech was very

00:00:23.616 --> 00:00:24.693
slurred.

00:00:24.693 --> 00:00:25.613
I struggled with it

00:00:25.613 --> 00:00:26.810
through high school.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.462
I somehow stopped writing with my

00:00:28.462 --> 00:00:30.173
right hand and taught myself how to

00:00:30.173 --> 00:00:31.420
write with my left hand.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:33.010
So, overcoming obstacles is

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.472
something that I'm used to

00:00:34.472 --> 00:00:35.750
finding solutions to.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.475
After business school, it became

00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:39.259
really, really tough to walk, and

00:00:39.259 --> 00:00:40.925
I remember walking 56th Avenue

00:00:40.925 --> 00:00:41.860
in New York City.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:43.723
And it's really a long block and

00:00:43.723 --> 00:00:46.000
those blocks would take 45 minutes.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.744
It just became really hard and

00:00:47.744 --> 00:00:49.766
I just started to look, what would

00:00:49.766 --> 00:00:51.800
my life in a wheelchair look like?

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:54.355
I'm a nerd, and

00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:56.350
this is a major theme of the story.

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:57.592
I built a spreadsheet and

00:00:57.592 --> 00:00:58.951
the spreadsheet was, what

00:00:58.951 --> 00:01:00.546
are the pros of brain surgery and

00:01:00.546 --> 00:01:02.330
what are the cons of brain surgery?

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:03.563
But I just sort of did the

00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.413
analysis, I realized I had nothing

00:01:05.413 --> 00:01:07.216
to lose but to have brain surgery.

00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:08.688
Eight weeks after,

00:01:08.688 --> 00:01:10.850
I was walking the same block,

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.061
56th Avenue,

00:01:12.061 --> 00:01:14.569
and I realized what was taking 45

00:01:14.569 --> 00:01:16.559
minutes took 4 minutes and

00:01:16.559 --> 00:01:18.202
I got to Sixth Avenue,

00:01:18.202 --> 00:01:20.215
still makes me emotional.

00:01:20.215 --> 00:01:21.638
I just feel like crying and

00:01:21.638 --> 00:01:22.479
laughing, and

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:24.355
I never felt that feeling probably

00:01:24.355 --> 00:01:25.850
since I was 15 years old.

00:01:29.585 --> 00:01:33.616
I started running December 4th,

00:01:33.616 --> 00:01:35.009
2006.

00:01:35.009 --> 00:01:36.299
For anyone who runs out there as

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:37.787
you know, you always remember your

00:01:37.787 --> 00:01:38.829
first race and you always

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:40.800
remember the escalation process.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.269
I did probably a couple more 10Ks,

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:45.576
and then I was, if I can do a 10K,

00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:47.423
why not do a half marathon?

00:01:47.423 --> 00:01:49.236
The thing with me when I'm running

00:01:49.236 --> 00:01:50.924
is I actually have to think about

00:01:50.924 --> 00:01:51.618
every step.

00:01:51.618 --> 00:01:53.096
So I have to consciously think,

00:01:53.096 --> 00:01:54.736
lay my foot flat, lay my foot flat,

00:01:54.736 --> 00:01:55.887
whereas most able bodied

00:01:55.887 --> 00:01:57.334
people just pitter-patter.

00:01:57.334 --> 00:02:00.121
[MUSIC]

00:02:00.121 --> 00:02:01.345
But I always have my sights on

00:02:01.345 --> 00:02:02.926
the greatest marathon in the world,

00:02:02.926 --> 00:02:03.691
which I believe is

00:02:03.691 --> 00:02:04.767
New York City Marathon.

00:02:04.767 --> 00:02:07.218
I did it in 2018, and it's probably

00:02:07.218 --> 00:02:09.165
really one of the best marathons

00:02:09.165 --> 00:02:10.402
you could ever run.

00:02:10.402 --> 00:02:13.388
That was just a dream come true.

00:02:13.388 --> 00:02:16.303
I had written about in my GSB essay

00:02:16.303 --> 00:02:18.906
that my dream in life was to one

00:02:18.906 --> 00:02:20.687
day run a marathon.

00:02:20.687 --> 00:02:22.973
It has truly been remarkable

00:02:22.973 --> 00:02:25.450
what science has done to change

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.307
my life and

00:02:26.307 --> 00:02:28.889
to give me the miracle of running

00:02:28.889 --> 00:02:32.635
[MUSIC]

00:02:32.635 --> 00:02:34.442
I always tell myself every morning

00:02:34.442 --> 00:02:36.006
that I have a superhero cape.

00:02:36.006 --> 00:02:38.056
And for me, woman's health, which

00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:40.109
is super interesting because we've

00:02:40.109 --> 00:02:42.162
been fighting for representation in

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:43.940
the healthcare field.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.790
The beauty about Telehealth is you

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.382
can do it in the privacy of your

00:02:47.382 --> 00:02:48.147
living room,

00:02:48.147 --> 00:02:50.061
you can really tell your healthcare

00:02:50.061 --> 00:02:51.970
needs n a non-judgmental way.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:54.009
I've made a conscious decision to

00:02:54.009 --> 00:02:55.836
use my platform to talk about,

00:02:55.836 --> 00:02:57.664
how this affects marginalized

00:02:57.664 --> 00:02:58.589
communities.

00:02:58.589 --> 00:03:00.560
The people who are usually going to

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:02.531
get caught in the fray are people

00:03:02.531 --> 00:03:04.137
of color, people of lower

00:03:04.137 --> 00:03:06.181
economic societies, and people

00:03:06.181 --> 00:03:08.590
in marginalized communities i.e.,

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:09.979
transgender people.

00:03:09.979 --> 00:03:11.985
Simple Health is a reproductive

00:03:11.985 --> 00:03:13.620
care company which offers

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:14.958
services to women and

00:03:14.958 --> 00:03:16.675
people born with ovaries.

00:03:16.675 --> 00:03:18.019
Right now we currently do

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:18.915
birth control.

00:03:18.915 --> 00:03:20.028
So you come online,

00:03:20.028 --> 00:03:21.731
you go on simplehealth.com,

00:03:21.731 --> 00:03:23.826
you figure out online consultation,

00:03:23.826 --> 00:03:25.659
a doctor reviews it, he gets back

00:03:25.659 --> 00:03:27.308
to you with your medication.

00:03:27.308 --> 00:03:29.062
And then we send to the pharmacy

00:03:29.062 --> 00:03:30.343
and you should get your

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.302
prescription within three to five

00:03:32.302 --> 00:03:34.125
days, all discreetly from your

00:03:34.125 --> 00:03:35.947
living room with a judgment free

00:03:35.947 --> 00:03:37.450
doctor's appointment.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.476
So again, we're super

00:03:38.476 --> 00:03:39.147
proud of that.

00:03:39.147 --> 00:03:40.926
[MUSIC]

00:03:40.926 --> 00:03:42.493
So really having a business around

00:03:42.493 --> 00:03:43.682
longitudinal care around

00:03:43.682 --> 00:03:45.370
reproductive health.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:46.827
No one's done it yet, but

00:03:46.827 --> 00:03:48.351
I tell people all the time,

00:03:48.351 --> 00:03:50.206
this is what the GSB does for you,

00:03:50.206 --> 00:03:52.074
makes you dream as big as you can.

00:03:52.074 --> 00:03:53.936
My thesis at Simple Health is that

00:03:53.936 --> 00:03:55.665
we have to keep on continuing to

00:03:55.665 --> 00:03:57.326
fight for people's rights to

00:03:57.326 --> 00:03:59.386
privacy of their healthcare needs,

00:03:59.386 --> 00:04:00.924
reproductive care or not.

00:04:00.924 --> 00:04:03.209
One of the things I'd always said

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:05.166
to myself is when I grow up and

00:04:05.166 --> 00:04:07.450
be a great CEO, I'm really going to

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:09.491
try and create a company where

00:04:09.491 --> 00:04:11.696
the employee workforce matches

00:04:11.696 --> 00:04:14.380
the patient or the consumer base.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:16.410
I am super proud to say that we

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.870
are 47% people of color.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.134
We are 46% LGBTQIA+, we're 36%

00:04:22.134 --> 00:04:25.686
disabled, we're 10% single parents,

00:04:25.686 --> 00:04:27.896
and we're 6% veterans.

00:04:27.896 --> 00:04:30.160
We don't do this by checking boxes,

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.475
we just actually do it

00:04:31.475 --> 00:04:33.528
by attracting the right talent.

00:04:33.528 --> 00:04:34.965
[MUSIC]

00:04:34.965 --> 00:04:36.214
I had always wanted to go

00:04:36.214 --> 00:04:37.775
to business school since I was

00:04:37.775 --> 00:04:39.398
probably seven years old, and

00:04:39.398 --> 00:04:40.898
I think my parents thought,

00:04:40.898 --> 00:04:42.350
what is she talking about?

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.814
We were living in Trinidad,

00:04:43.814 --> 00:04:45.156
how does she even know what

00:04:45.156 --> 00:04:46.257
business school is?

00:04:46.257 --> 00:04:47.713
[MUSIC]

00:04:47.713 --> 00:04:50.038
In terms of what Stanford has done

00:04:50.038 --> 00:04:52.363
for me, it has just surpassed any

00:04:52.363 --> 00:04:54.939
expectation that I've ever thought

00:04:54.939 --> 00:04:57.530
about, has changed my entire life.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.861
The way I communicate has made me

00:04:59.861 --> 00:05:01.507
the leader I am today.

00:05:01.507 --> 00:05:02.822
The power of leadership,

00:05:02.822 --> 00:05:04.136
the power of empathy, and

00:05:04.136 --> 00:05:06.080
really the power of communication.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.530
So professionally that's what I

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:08.051
took away.

00:05:08.051 --> 00:05:10.192
Personally just an incredible

00:05:10.192 --> 00:05:11.845
amount of friends and,

00:05:11.845 --> 00:05:17.709
[MUSIC]

00:05:17.709 --> 00:05:19.924
I had such a great bunch of

00:05:19.924 --> 00:05:22.985
classmates that never made me feel

00:05:22.985 --> 00:05:24.152
different.

00:05:24.152 --> 00:05:25.281
[MUSIC]

00:05:25.281 --> 00:05:26.430
Never made me feel,

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.054
[MUSIC]

00:05:29.054 --> 00:05:30.328
Disabled.

00:05:30.328 --> 00:05:33.389
But I think because of that is

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:36.324
really why I can be the great

00:05:36.324 --> 00:05:39.641
leader I am today or the person I

00:05:39.641 --> 00:05:42.843
